archmage84 wrote:
>
> I guess my point is that non-weapon skills seem to be the only ones
> worth advancing, considering that even a x2 multiplier from an
> artifact for the damage is an extra _four_ damage. Whoop de do. I
> think you'll find that the #twoweapon command is not overpowered, it's
> that the weapon skills are underpowered. What do you think?

On the one hand I think skill slots have more benefits than you are
currently considering.

On the other hand skill slots are a limited resource.

It's tempting to #enhance melee weapons to Expert as soon as possible. 
That's a trap.  Melee is under powered and over-risked compared to other
combat methods.  Unless you have a built a table of possible options
given the 29 possible slots, plus a consideration that more XP levels
give harder monsters so more XP levels are not always the best plan,
moving a melee weapon to Expert is a bad idea until other classes are
covered.

It's tempting to #enhance all classes as offered.  Several character
classes can enhance a lot more classes than they can use.  In Nethack
game play specialists tend to beat generalists.  Yet in takes being a
generalist game to game to learn how to be a specialist within any one
game.

For character classes that can multi-fire missile weapons, skill class
enhancement is huge.  Being able to throw d3 daggers when Expert in
dagger beats even #twoweapon melee.  If your character class can get to
Expert in dagger class it IS a good idea to use daggers as much as
possible, to get to Expert soonest, and to gather up a stack of elven
daggers then highly enchant them.  For these classes I tend to exit the
mines Expert in dagger and using my stacks like machine guns.  I get the
characters to expert before I have even built up enough elven daggers to
have dropped my other dagger types.  Even wizards benefit by moving
dagger to expert extremely soon.

For spell schools the biggest benefit is moving to Skilled.  It chances
the behavior of several types of spells so it should be carefully
considered.  Moving a spell school to Expert might just cost slots
without improving a failure rate that's already 0%.  But for wizards
they have the slots to spare so I tend to run my wizards to max in every
school I can.  But for wizards I might not enhance any weapon slot at
all other than dagger and some good artifact weapon that I use as an
alternate to Magicbane when I've gotten myself into trouble.


